WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:36.986 --> 00:00:37.686
Pirates.

00:00:37.946 --> 00:00:40.008
They range from scary to hilarious.

00:00:40.588 --> 00:00:46.292
Famous tales have been told for decades,
from real-life pirates like Blackbeard to

00:00:46.372 --> 00:00:47.493
Captain Jack Sparrow

00:00:47.813 --> 00:00:49.534
and the Pirates of the Caribbean.

00:00:50.154 --> 00:00:54.998
This documentary dives into the facts and
history, including the golden age of

00:00:55.038 --> 00:00:56.799
pirates, during the 17th

00:00:56.839 --> 00:01:00.923
century, much of which took place in the
famous Caribbean islands.

00:01:01.804 --> 00:01:07.330
In this edition of DOC TV's monster horror
documentary series, we'll look at pirates,

00:01:07.610 --> 00:01:08.411
riches and

00:01:08.491 --> 00:01:09.312
treasure hunting.

00:01:10.953 --> 00:01:17.834
Ahoy mateys, let's get sailing Piracy
emerged from and reflected,

00:01:18.254 --> 00:01:23.398
on a smaller scale, disputes over trade
and colonization among the competing

00:01:23.498 --> 00:01:25.140
European powers of the era,

00:01:25.760 --> 00:01:30.604
including the kingdoms of Spain, Britain,
the Netherlands, France and Portugal.

00:01:31.445 --> 00:01:37.089
Most pirates in this period were of Welsh,
Dutch, English, French and Irish origin.

00:01:37.830 --> 00:01:42.834
Numerous pirates came from impoverished
urban environments exploring ways to make

00:01:42.934 --> 00:01:44.135
money and reprieve.

00:01:44.836 --> 00:01:49.438
London in particular, was known for
increased unemployment, poverty and

00:01:49.498 --> 00:01:51.659
crowding, which forced individuals to

00:01:51.739 --> 00:01:52.439
piracy.

00:01:52.999 --> 00:01:55.900
Piracy also delivered power and quick
riches.

00:01:56.781 --> 00:02:02.103
Historians like John Fisk indicate the
start of the golden age of piracy at

00:02:02.123 --> 00:02:03.363
around 1650,

00:02:03.744 --> 00:02:08.526
when the end of the wars of religion
allowed European countries to continue the

00:02:08.566 --> 00:02:10.126
expansion of their colonial

00:02:10.266 --> 00:02:10.966
empires.

00:02:11.707 --> 00:02:14.168
This involved significant seaborne trade.

00:02:14.612 --> 00:02:16.713
and an overall economic improvement.

00:02:17.293 --> 00:02:20.795
Money was to be made or stolen and much
trekked by ship.

00:02:21.695 --> 00:02:27.798
French buccaneers established themselves
on northern Hispaniola as early as 1625,

00:02:28.298 --> 00:02:28.998
but

00:02:28.998 --> 00:02:31.919
lived mainly as hunters of pigs and cattle
rather than robbers.

00:02:32.500 --> 00:02:37.242
Their shift to full-time piracy was
incremental and somewhat motivated by

00:02:37.302 --> 00:02:39.402
Spanish measures to wipe out both

00:02:39.422 --> 00:02:42.924
the buccaneers and the prey animals on
which they relied.

00:02:43.428 --> 00:02:47.290
The buccaneers' migration from
Hispaniola's landmass to the more

00:02:47.330 --> 00:02:50.312
defensible offshore island of Tortuga
restricted

00:02:50.352 --> 00:02:53.694
their resources and accelerated their
piratical raids.

00:02:54.355 --> 00:02:59.278
According to Alexander Exquemlin, a
buccaneer and historian who remains a

00:02:59.298 --> 00:03:01.119
decisive source on this period,

00:03:01.779 --> 00:03:06.582
the Tortuga buccaneer Pierre Le Grand
pioneered the settlers' attacks on

00:03:06.662 --> 00:03:08.764
Galleons, making the return journey

00:03:08.804 --> 00:03:09.504
to Spain.

00:03:09.932 --> 00:03:16.116
The English capture of Jamaica from Spain
in 1655 boosted the growth of buccaneering

00:03:16.316 --> 00:03:17.016
on

00:03:17.016 --> 00:03:17.516
Tortuga.

00:03:18.058 --> 00:03:22.681
The early English governors of Jamaica
voluntarily gave letters of marque to

00:03:22.761 --> 00:03:25.002
Tortuga buccaneers and their fellow

00:03:25.103 --> 00:03:25.803
citizens.

00:03:26.383 --> 00:03:31.227
At the same time, the development of Port
Royal supplied these raiders with a far

00:03:31.287 --> 00:03:33.368
more beneficial and gratifying place

00:03:33.388 --> 00:03:34.349
to sell their booty.

00:03:35.109 --> 00:03:41.494
In the 1660s, the new French governor of
Tortuga Bertrand d'Augeron also provided

00:03:41.574 --> 00:03:46.477
privateering commissions to his colonists
and English cutthroats from Port Royal.

00:03:47.318 --> 00:03:51.081
These circumstances brought Caribbean
buccaneering to its peak.

00:03:51.981 --> 00:03:57.045
Several elements caused Anglo-American
pirates, some of whom had been introduced

00:03:57.046 --> 00:03:58.846
to piracy during the buccaneering

00:03:58.886 --> 00:04:04.150
period, to look beyond the Caribbean for
treasure as the 1690s began.

00:04:04.771 --> 00:04:09.554
The end of Britain's Stuart years had
revived the traditional animosity between

00:04:09.654 --> 00:04:11.735
France and Britain, terminating

00:04:11.755 --> 00:04:16.279
the fruitful cooperation between English
Jamaica and French Tortuga.

00:04:17.179 --> 00:04:23.023
The destruction of Port Royal by an
earthquake in 1692 further decreased the

00:04:23.063 --> 00:04:23.763
Caribbean's

00:04:23.804 --> 00:04:28.447
interests by destroying the pirates'
principal market for the stolen loot.

00:04:29.408 --> 00:04:34.451
Caribbean colonial governments started to
dump the traditional policy of no peace

00:04:34.531 --> 00:04:35.412
beyond the line.

00:04:35.848 --> 00:04:40.849
under which it was comprehended that war
would continue and thus letters of marque

00:04:41.109 --> 00:04:42.770
would be bestowed in the Caribbean

00:04:43.150 --> 00:04:45.551
regardless of peace treaties signed in
Europe.

00:04:46.131 --> 00:04:50.412
Subsequently, commissions would be
provided only in wartime and their

00:04:50.472 --> 00:04:52.913
restrictions would be rigorously executed.

00:04:53.653 --> 00:04:56.494
Also, much of the Spanish main had been
depleted.

00:04:57.034 --> 00:05:03.435
Maracaibo alone had been plundered three
times between 1667 and 1670-80,

00:05:03.876 --> 00:05:08.181
while Rio de la Hacha had been raided five
times and Tulu eight.

00:05:08.921 --> 00:05:14.742
At the same time, England's less favoured
colonies, including New York, Bermuda and

00:05:14.823 --> 00:05:15.523
Rhode Island,

00:05:15.883 --> 00:05:18.604
had become cash-starved by the Navigation
Acts.

00:05:19.404 --> 00:05:24.185
Vendors and governors enthusiastic for
cash were inclined to overlook and even

00:05:24.285 --> 00:05:25.825
support pirate voyages.

00:05:26.606 --> 00:05:31.707
One colonial administrator defended a
pirate because he thought it very harsh to

00:05:31.767 --> 00:05:33.368
hang people that brings in

00:05:33.508 --> 00:05:34.908
gold to these provinces.

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:40.361
Although some pirates working out of New
England and the middle colonies targeted

00:05:40.441 --> 00:05:42.422
Spain's more remote Pacific coast

00:05:42.462 --> 00:05:48.764
colonies well into the 1690s and beyond,
the Indian Ocean was a wealthier and more

00:05:48.804 --> 00:05:49.504
attractive

00:05:49.504 --> 00:05:50.104
target.

00:05:50.524 --> 00:05:55.946
India's economic output dwarfed Europe's
during this time, especially in high-value

00:05:56.026 --> 00:05:57.387
luxury products like

00:05:57.427 --> 00:06:00.688
calico and silk, which made perfect pirate
booty.

00:06:01.308 --> 00:06:06.653
At the same time, No effective navies
sailed the Indian Ocean, leaving both

00:06:06.733 --> 00:06:08.135
local shipping and the

00:06:08.155 --> 00:06:12.139
diverse East India Company's crafts
vulnerable to invasion.

00:06:13.201 --> 00:06:17.766
This set the stage for the renowned
pirates of Thomas II, Robert Culliford,

00:06:18.106 --> 00:06:20.069
Henry Every, and, although

00:06:20.109 --> 00:06:22.692
his guilt stays contentious, William Kidd.

00:06:24.321 --> 00:06:29.785
Peace treaties concluded the War of the
Spanish Succession in 1713 and

00:06:29.786 --> 00:06:31.427
1714.

00:06:32.107 --> 00:06:36.991
As a result, thousands of seamen,
including European privateers who had

00:06:37.111 --> 00:06:39.013
served in the West Indies, were

00:06:39.093 --> 00:06:43.497
rid of military commitment when the
cross-Atlantic colonial shipping trade

00:06:43.757 --> 00:06:44.978
started to explode.

00:06:45.538 --> 00:06:50.803
In addition, European seafarers who had
been driven by unemployment to work on

00:06:50.863 --> 00:06:51.783
board merchantmen,

00:06:52.344 --> 00:06:57.359
including slave vessels, were frequently
eager to leave that profession and turn to

00:06:57.419 --> 00:06:58.980
pirating, giving pirate

00:06:59.040 --> 00:07:04.243
captains a constant pool of draftees on
miscellaneous coasts across the Atlantic.

00:07:05.043 --> 00:07:10.767
In 1715, pirates tackled a significant
attack on Spanish divers trying to

00:07:10.787 --> 00:07:11.647
retrieve gold

00:07:12.007 --> 00:07:15.770
from the Sumcombe treasure galleon Orca de
Lima near Florida.

00:07:16.390 --> 00:07:21.433
The heart of the pirate squad was a group
of English ex-privateers, all of whom

00:07:21.737 --> 00:07:23.217
would shortly be exalted in

00:07:23.337 --> 00:07:24.037
infamy.

00:07:24.318 --> 00:07:30.440
Henry Jennings, Benjamin Hornigold, Samuel
Bellamy of wider galley fame, Edward

00:07:30.540 --> 00:07:31.240
England

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:32.360
and Charles Vane.

00:07:32.920 --> 00:07:37.842
The raid was triumphant, but contrary to
their expectations, the governor of

00:07:37.882 --> 00:07:39.482
Jamaica declined to permit

00:07:39.582 --> 00:07:42.883
Jennings and his mates to spend their loot
on his island.

00:07:43.723 --> 00:07:49.461
With Kingston and the declining Port Royal
sealed to them, Jennings, Hornigold, and

00:07:49.481 --> 00:07:50.402
their mates based

00:07:50.403 --> 00:07:54.505
themselves at Nassau, on the island of New
Providence in the Bahamas.

00:07:55.425 --> 00:07:59.688
Nassau would become a base for these
pirates and their many draftees until the

00:07:59.708 --> 00:08:01.990
arrival of Governor Woods Rogers in

00:08:01.991 --> 00:08:06.833
1718, which signalled the cessation of the
Republic of Pirates.

00:08:07.634 --> 00:08:12.577
Rogers and other British governors had the
power to absolve pirates under the King's

00:08:12.697 --> 00:08:14.318
Act of Grace, while

00:08:14.358 --> 00:08:17.861
Hornigold accepted this pardon to become a
pirate others.

00:08:18.557 --> 00:08:21.878
like Blackbeard, returned to piracy
following their acquittal.

00:08:22.678 --> 00:08:27.820
Transatlantic shipping traffic between the
Caribbean Africa and Europe started to

00:08:27.900 --> 00:08:29.480
skyrocket in the 18th

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:35.042
century, a model known as the triangular
trade and became an affluent target for

00:08:35.102 --> 00:08:35.802
piracy.

00:08:36.262 --> 00:08:41.724
Trade ships voyages from Europe to the
African coast, trading fabricated goods

00:08:41.744 --> 00:08:43.184
and weapons for enslaved

00:08:43.244 --> 00:08:43.944
people.

00:08:44.564 --> 00:08:48.894
The traders then journeyed to the
Caribbean to sell the slaves and return to

00:08:48.974 --> 00:08:51.274
Europe with merchandise like sugar,

00:08:51.634 --> 00:08:52.975
cocoa and tobacco.

00:08:53.655 --> 00:08:58.696
In another triangular trade route, ships
would carry preserved cod, raw materials

00:08:59.036 --> 00:09:00.097
and rum to Europe,

00:09:00.537 --> 00:09:05.478
where a part of the shipment would be sold
for fabricated goods, which, along with

00:09:05.498 --> 00:09:07.519
the rest of the initial shipment, were

00:09:07.539 --> 00:09:12.300
then hauled to the Caribbean, where they
were traded for molasses and sugar, which

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:13.840
were then borne to New England.

00:09:14.521 --> 00:09:18.482
Ships in the triangular trade frequently
made money at each location.

00:09:19.143 --> 00:09:23.965
As a component of the settlement of the
War of the Spanish Succession, the British

00:09:24.145 --> 00:09:26.026
South Sea Company received the

00:09:26.066 --> 00:09:31.209
Haciento, a Spanish government agreement
to provide slaves to Spain's New World

00:09:31.289 --> 00:09:32.570
colonies, which

00:09:32.650 --> 00:09:37.313
furnished British traders and smugglers
more access to formerly closed Spanish

00:09:37.373 --> 00:09:38.473
markets in America.

00:09:39.414 --> 00:09:44.197
This agreement also contributed heavily to
the spread of piracy across the Western

00:09:44.237 --> 00:09:44.937
Atlantic.

00:09:45.317 --> 00:09:49.579
Shipping to the colonies accompanied the
surge of skilled mariners after the war.

00:09:50.359 --> 00:09:55.682
Merchant shippers utilized the labor
surplus to push wages down, cut comas to

00:09:55.742 --> 00:09:57.182
maximize earnings, and

00:09:57.242 --> 00:10:00.444
create unfortunate circumstances aboard
their ships.

00:10:01.144 --> 00:10:05.426
Merchant sailors suffered death rates as
high or higher than the slaves being

00:10:05.466 --> 00:10:06.186
transported.

00:10:06.826 --> 00:10:11.028
Living conditions were so flawed that many
sailors started to choose a freer

00:10:11.088 --> 00:10:12.449
lifestyle as pirates.

00:10:13.173 --> 00:10:18.177
The expanded volume of shipping traffic
also could support a large body of pirates

00:10:18.257 --> 00:10:19.077
preying upon it.

00:10:20.038 --> 00:10:25.462
Many pirates had originally been sailors
for privateersmen, the Royal Navy or

00:10:25.522 --> 00:10:26.923
merchant seamen during this

00:10:26.963 --> 00:10:27.663
time.

00:10:27.963 --> 00:10:32.707
Most pirates had experience living on the
ocean and knew how difficult the

00:10:32.787 --> 00:10:33.988
circumstances could be.

00:10:34.848 --> 00:10:39.752
Sailors for the king usually have very
little to eat while on the sea and end up

00:10:39.852 --> 00:10:41.653
starving, sick and dying.

00:10:42.345 --> 00:10:47.249
That resulted in some sailors rejecting
the king and becoming pirates instead.

00:10:47.810 --> 00:10:50.913
This also permitted pirates to combat the
navy better.

00:10:51.633 --> 00:10:56.598
Unlike other seamen, pirates had rigid
rules for how they were treated on the

00:10:56.618 --> 00:10:57.318
craft.

00:10:57.639 --> 00:11:02.543
Contrary to popular belief, pirate
captains did not have a dictatorship over

00:11:02.544 --> 00:11:04.485
the rest of the pirates on their vessels.

00:11:05.045 --> 00:11:09.249
Captains had to be voted in and had rigid
regulations to observe.

00:11:09.801 --> 00:11:13.945
The captain was not treated better than
the other crewmates and was expected to

00:11:14.005 --> 00:11:15.486
treat the gang respectfully.

00:11:16.107 --> 00:11:20.350
This was an intended difference to
merchant captains, who usually treated

00:11:20.390 --> 00:11:21.391
their crews terribly.

00:11:21.411 --> 00:11:23.133
Many pirates had previously been treated
as if they were the ones who were the most

00:11:23.134 --> 00:11:23.834
important.

00:11:23.834 --> 00:11:28.195
operated on these merchants' vessels and
knew how awful some captains could be.

00:11:28.975 --> 00:11:33.697
As a result, vessels often enforced
councils comprising all crew members.

00:11:34.558 --> 00:11:39.100
Some councils were employed daily to
create everyday decisions, while others

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:40.980
were utilized as a court system

00:11:41.501 --> 00:11:44.802
only when criminal happenings or legal
matters called for it.

00:11:45.382 --> 00:11:50.444
Whatever the issue, crew members on pirate
ships usually have as much authority as

00:11:50.464 --> 00:11:52.105
the captain outside of battle.

00:11:52.713 --> 00:11:57.456
The captain only had complete control in
times of conflict and could be removed

00:11:57.516 --> 00:11:59.438
from this position if he showed

00:11:59.498 --> 00:12:01.739
coerced in the face of the adversary.

00:12:02.280 --> 00:12:04.301
He was also fearless in battle.

00:12:04.941 --> 00:12:08.704
The pirates did not want things to end up
the same as on a navy ship.

00:12:09.645 --> 00:12:15.429
John Taylor, Olivia Levasseur, Christopher
Condon and Edward England operated from

00:12:15.489 --> 00:12:16.189
Madagascar

00:12:16.469 --> 00:12:19.732
between 1719 and 1721.

00:12:20.512 --> 00:12:24.859
Taylor and Levasseur reaped the most
significant prize in the history of the

00:12:24.919 --> 00:12:27.141
Golden Age of Parasy, the

00:12:27.181 --> 00:12:34.066
plunder of the Portuguese East in diamond,
Nosso Senhora do Cabo, at Ranean in 1721,

00:12:34.446 --> 00:12:36.688
looting diamonds and other treasures.

00:12:37.408 --> 00:12:41.491
Condent was also a triumphant pirate, but
England was not.

00:12:42.312 --> 00:12:48.957
He was abandoned on Comoros by Taylor and
Levasseur in 1721, and died soon after

00:12:48.977 --> 00:12:49.677
that.

00:12:49.769 --> 00:12:54.673
Despite the conquest of Tela and
Levasseur, the pirate round fast decreased

00:12:54.693 --> 00:12:55.393
again.

00:12:56.074 --> 00:13:00.378
The Barbary pirates were pirates and
privateers that worked from the North

00:13:00.458 --> 00:13:03.040
African, also known as the Barbary coast,

00:13:03.601 --> 00:13:09.246
ports of Algiers, Salé, Morocco, Tunis,
and Tripoli, preying on shipping in the

00:13:09.286 --> 00:13:10.447
western Mediterranean

00:13:10.567 --> 00:13:15.491
Sea from the ears of the crusades and
ships on their route to Asia around Africa

00:13:15.951 --> 00:13:17.372
until the early 19th

00:13:17.433 --> 00:13:18.133
century.

00:13:18.421 --> 00:13:23.442
They repeatedly assailed the coastal
villages and towns of Italy, Spain and

00:13:23.522 --> 00:13:24.863
Mediterranean islands,

00:13:25.303 --> 00:13:30.044
and their inhabitants almost completely
abandoned long stretches of the Italian

00:13:30.144 --> 00:13:31.285
and Spanish coasts.

00:13:31.945 --> 00:13:37.566
Since the 17th century, Barbary pirates
sometimes penetrated the Atlantic and

00:13:37.666 --> 00:13:38.887
struck as far north

00:13:38.967 --> 00:13:39.687
as Iceland.

00:13:40.307 --> 00:13:46.893
According to Robert Davis, between 1
million and 1.25 million Europeans were

00:13:46.913 --> 00:13:52.375
arrested by Barbary pirates and sold as
slaves in Northern Africa between the 16th

00:13:52.495 --> 00:13:53.855
and 19th centuries.

00:13:54.575 --> 00:14:00.257
Barbary pirates prospered in the early
17th century as new sailing rigs by Simon

00:14:00.258 --> 00:14:00.958
de Danza

00:14:01.277 --> 00:14:06.158
enabled North African raiders, for the
first time, to brave the Atlantic and

00:14:06.258 --> 00:14:07.499
Mediterranean waters.

00:14:08.259 --> 00:14:12.520
More than 20,000 prisoners were said to be
jailed in Algiers alone.

00:14:13.120 --> 00:14:15.321
The rich were permitted to redeem
themselves.

00:14:15.785 --> 00:14:17.926
but the poor were sentenced to slavery.

00:14:18.566 --> 00:14:23.267
Their masters would, sometimes allow them
to secure freedom by pledging Islam.

00:14:23.987 --> 00:14:29.129
Numerous people of good social position
Italians, Germans, English travelers and

00:14:29.209 --> 00:14:30.609
Spaniards in the south were

00:14:30.649 --> 00:14:31.809
captives for a while.

00:14:32.450 --> 00:14:37.831
In 1627 Iceland was subject to raids known
as the Turkish Abductions.

00:14:38.631 --> 00:14:45.357
Nur-at-Ris is believed to have taken 400
detainees, 242 of the prisoners, were

00:14:45.397 --> 00:14:48.199
later sold into slavery on the Barbary
coast.

00:14:48.779 --> 00:14:52.681
The pirates seized only young people and
those in good health.

00:14:53.302 --> 00:14:58.024
All those offering opposition were slain,
and the older adults were collected in a

00:14:58.064 --> 00:14:59.485
church set on fire.

00:15:00.146 --> 00:15:02.387
Among those arrested was Oliver E.

00:15:02.427 --> 00:15:08.050
Gilson, who was ransomed the next year
and, upon returning to Iceland, documented

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:09.411
a slave record about

00:15:09.451 --> 00:15:10.391
his experience.

00:15:11.132 --> 00:15:14.954
Another famous captive from that raid was
Gurdjiluf's Simonar daughter.

00:15:15.574 --> 00:15:20.277
The sack of Vestmannaigar is known in the
history of Iceland as Tegcharranid.

00:15:20.917 --> 00:15:26.120
While pirates are commonly represented
with eye patches, this myth originated in

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:27.701
19th century novels

00:15:27.841 --> 00:15:32.083
and tales of buccaneers, including
compensation for a lost eye.

00:15:32.624 --> 00:15:37.747
Few historical pirates wore patches over
their eyes, but some, like the 18th

00:15:37.787 --> 00:15:39.528
century Arab pirate Roma

00:15:39.708 --> 00:15:41.869
Ibn Jabba al-Jalahima did.

00:15:42.833 --> 00:15:47.517
While the golden age of European and
American pirates is typically thought to

00:15:47.537 --> 00:15:48.958
have concluded between

00:15:48.959 --> 00:15:55.663
1710 and 1730, the prosperity of the
Barbary pirates persisted until the early

00:15:56.183 --> 00:15:56.883
19th century.

00:15:57.504 --> 00:16:02.648
Unlike the European powers, the young
United States declined to pay tribute to

00:16:02.649 --> 00:16:04.129
the Barbary states and

00:16:04.249 --> 00:16:08.993
answered with the First Barbary War and
the Second Barbary War against North

00:16:09.093 --> 00:16:09.793
Africa.

00:16:09.941 --> 00:16:13.825
when the Bargary pirates seized and
enslaved American seafarers.

00:16:14.305 --> 00:16:19.369
By the early 18th century, all nations'
tolerance toward privateers was declining.

00:16:20.070 --> 00:16:25.074
After the Treaty of Utrecht was signed,
the surplus of trained sailors without a

00:16:25.114 --> 00:16:27.035
job was both a godsend and

00:16:27.055 --> 00:16:28.655
a scourge for all pirates.

00:16:29.256 --> 00:16:33.358
Initially, the abundance of men had
multiplied the number of pirates.

00:16:34.238 --> 00:16:39.020
This inevitably led to the raiding of more
vessels, which significantly weakened

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:40.661
trade for all European

00:16:40.741 --> 00:16:41.441
nations.

00:16:41.841 --> 00:16:47.244
In response, European countries supported
their navies to present merchants with

00:16:47.304 --> 00:16:48.724
greater safety and hunt

00:16:48.825 --> 00:16:49.645
down pirates.

00:16:50.357 --> 00:16:55.458
The surplus of skilled seafarers meant a
large pool could also be drafted into

00:16:55.559 --> 00:16:56.459
national navies.

00:16:57.119 --> 00:17:02.100
The latter half of 1718, including the
arrival of Governor Woods Rogers in

00:17:02.180 --> 00:17:03.681
Nassau, represents a

00:17:03.741 --> 00:17:07.402
turning point in the history of piracy in
the New World.

00:17:08.222 --> 00:17:13.084
Without a safe base and with increasing
pressure from naval forces, the rovers

00:17:13.244 --> 00:17:14.364
lost their momentum.

00:17:15.064 --> 00:17:17.525
The appeal of the Spanish treasures had
vanished.

00:17:18.109 --> 00:17:20.151
and the hunters slowly became the hunted.

00:17:21.011 --> 00:17:24.895
By early 1719, the remaining pirates were
on the run.

00:17:25.635 --> 00:17:30.459
Many of them journeyed toward West Africa,
seizing inadequately protected slavers.

00:17:31.300 --> 00:17:36.204
The heralded golden age of piracy didn't
last a decade, but tales of

00:17:36.284 --> 00:17:38.005
larger-than-life pirates have

00:17:38.045 --> 00:17:39.446
endured the test of time.

00:17:40.207 --> 00:17:44.991
We've mentioned Blackbeard several times
because he is, without question, one of

00:17:44.992 --> 00:17:47.033
the most infamous pirates who ever

00:17:47.093 --> 00:17:47.793
lived.

00:17:48.357 --> 00:17:51.598
Now, let's dig a bit deeper into the man
and his legacy.

00:17:52.399 --> 00:17:56.681
Edward Teach, alternatively known as
Edward Thatch and better known as

00:17:56.741 --> 00:17:59.362
Blackbeard, was an English pirate who

00:17:59.442 --> 00:18:03.624
worked around the West Indies and the
eastern coast of Britain's North American

00:18:03.704 --> 00:18:04.404
territories.

00:18:05.104 --> 00:18:10.026
Little is known about his early life, but
he may have been a seafarer on privateer

00:18:10.086 --> 00:18:11.807
vessels during Queen Anne's

00:18:11.907 --> 00:18:16.109
War before he decided on the Bahamian
island of New Providence.

00:18:16.509 --> 00:18:22.294
a base for Captain Benjamin Hornigold,
whose crew teach Halide with around 1716.

00:18:22.895 --> 00:18:28.239
Hornigold placed him in power of a sloop
that he had seized, and the two took part

00:18:28.259 --> 00:18:29.400
in multiple acts of

00:18:29.480 --> 00:18:30.180
piracy.

00:18:30.702 --> 00:18:34.725
Their fleet was increased by the addition
of two more ships, one of which was

00:18:34.785 --> 00:18:37.107
controlled by Steed Bonnet, but

00:18:37.167 --> 00:18:43.573
Hornigold withdrew from piracy toward the
end of 1717, taking two boats with him.

00:18:44.401 --> 00:18:49.245
Teach seized a French slave ship, Le
Concorde, renamed her Queen Anne's

00:18:49.325 --> 00:18:51.127
Revenge, provided her with

00:18:51.187 --> 00:18:54.470
40 guns and crewed her with over 300 men.

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:56.932
He became a famed pirate.

00:18:57.472 --> 00:19:01.776
His nickname derived from his fearsome
appearance and thick black beard.

00:19:02.697 --> 00:19:07.100
He was documented to have tied lit fuses
under his hat to scare his enemies.

00:19:07.821 --> 00:19:12.565
He joined with pirates and secured the
port of Charlestown, South Carolina.

00:19:13.093 --> 00:19:14.714
ransoming the harbour's residents.

00:19:15.414 --> 00:19:19.937
He then ran Queen Anne's Revenge aground
on a sandbar near Beaufort, North

00:19:20.017 --> 00:19:20.717
Carolina.

00:19:21.278 --> 00:19:26.481
He parted company with Steed Bonnet and
settled in Bath, North Carolina, also

00:19:26.561 --> 00:19:28.262
known as Bathtown, where

00:19:28.282 --> 00:19:29.882
he obtained a royal pardon.

00:19:30.363 --> 00:19:35.526
However, he was soon back at sea, where he
drew the attention of Alexander Spotswood,

00:19:36.006 --> 00:19:37.207
the governor of Virginia.

00:19:37.907 --> 00:19:41.429
Spotswood organised a party of sailors and
soldiers to capture him.

00:19:42.045 --> 00:19:48.950
On November 22nd, 1718, after a fierce
battle, Teach and several of

00:19:48.951 --> 00:19:54.034
his crew were slain by a small force of
sailors led by Lieutenant Robert Maynard.

00:19:54.694 --> 00:19:59.317
He was allegedly stabbed 20 times and shot
five times before death.

00:19:59.958 --> 00:20:05.262
Teach was a savvy and calculating leader
who disregarded violence, relying instead

00:20:05.342 --> 00:20:06.843
on his fearsome image to

00:20:06.903 --> 00:20:09.805
evoke the reaction he wanted from those he
robbed.

00:20:10.657 --> 00:20:15.340
He was glorified after his death and
became the inspiration for an archetypal

00:20:15.440 --> 00:20:17.221
pirate in works of fiction

00:20:17.381 --> 00:20:18.561
across many genres.

00:20:19.262 --> 00:20:24.745
Since the end of the golden age of piracy,
Teach and his exploits have become the

00:20:24.765 --> 00:20:25.625
stuff of legend,

00:20:26.005 --> 00:20:30.028
inspiring books, films and even amusement
park rides.

00:20:30.788 --> 00:20:35.291
Much of what is known about him can be
sourced from Charles Johnson's A General

00:20:35.351 --> 00:20:37.672
History of the Robberies and Murders of

00:20:37.673 --> 00:20:39.413
the Most Notorious Pirates.

00:20:39.857 --> 00:20:46.499
published in Britain in 1724, a recognized
authority on the pirates of his time.

00:20:46.959 --> 00:20:51.900
Johnson's descriptions of such figures, as
Anne Bonny and Mary Read were for years,

00:20:52.360 --> 00:20:53.741
required reading for those

00:20:53.841 --> 00:20:55.201
interested in the subject.

00:20:56.041 --> 00:21:00.483
His stories fascinated readers, and the
second edition was quickly published.

00:21:01.043 --> 00:21:06.104
However, author Angus Constam suspects
that Johnson's entry on Blackbeard was

00:21:06.184 --> 00:21:07.585
colored a little to make a more

00:21:07.625 --> 00:21:08.845
sensational story.

00:21:09.469 --> 00:21:13.532
Her general history, though, is generally
considered to be a reliable source.

00:21:14.112 --> 00:21:16.554
Johnson may have been an assumed alias.

00:21:17.274 --> 00:21:21.197
As Johnson's accounts have been
corroborated in personal and official

00:21:21.277 --> 00:21:24.039
dispatches, Lee believes that whoever he
was,

00:21:24.299 --> 00:21:26.881
he had some access to official
correspondence.

00:21:27.641 --> 00:21:32.003
Constam speculates further, suggesting
that Johnson may have been the English

00:21:32.063 --> 00:21:34.364
playwright Charles Johnson, the British

00:21:34.424 --> 00:21:37.905
publisher Charles Rivington, or the writer
Daniel Defoe.

00:21:38.605 --> 00:21:44.868
In his 1951 work, The Great Days of
Parasy, author George Woodbury wrote that

00:21:44.928 --> 00:21:45.628
Johnson is

00:21:45.648 --> 00:21:51.150
obviously a pseudonym, continuing, one
cannot help suspecting that he may have

00:21:51.190 --> 00:21:52.511
been a pirate himself.

00:21:54.792 --> 00:21:59.794
Teach used black flags with death heads
and bloody flags to intimidate his

00:21:59.914 --> 00:22:00.614
enemies.

00:22:01.054 --> 00:22:05.275
Nevertheless, the flag, commonly
attributed to Blackbeard, depicting a

00:22:05.315 --> 00:22:07.936
horned skeleton spearing a heart while

00:22:07.976 --> 00:22:11.517
toasting the devil, was probably never
actually used by him.

00:22:12.497 --> 00:22:16.418
Despite his infamy, Teach was not the most
successful of pirates.

00:22:16.978 --> 00:22:22.540
Henry Every retired a rich man, and
Bartholomew Roberts took an estimated five

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:23.620
times the amount

00:22:23.800 --> 00:22:24.520
Teach stole.

00:22:26.021 --> 00:22:30.324
Treasure hunters have long busied
themselves searching for any trace of his

00:22:30.424 --> 00:22:32.685
rumored hoard of gold and silver, but

00:22:32.785 --> 00:22:37.429
nothing in the numerous sites explored
along the east coast of the US has ever

00:22:37.489 --> 00:22:38.510
been connected to him.

00:22:39.370 --> 00:22:43.774
Some tales suggest that pirates often
killed a prisoner on the spot where they

00:22:43.894 --> 00:22:46.096
buried their loot, and Teach is no

00:22:46.176 --> 00:22:51.159
exception in these stories, but that no
finds of counter-light is not exceptional.

00:22:51.680 --> 00:22:55.963
The buried pirate treasure is often
considered a modern myth for which almost

00:22:56.043 --> 00:22:58.105
no supporting evidence exists.

00:22:58.985 --> 00:23:03.588
The available records include nothing to
suggest that the burial of treasure was a

00:23:03.608 --> 00:23:05.670
common practice, except in the

00:23:05.710 --> 00:23:10.093
imaginations of the writers of fictional
accounts such as Treasure Island.

00:23:10.814 --> 00:23:15.337
Such holds would necessitate a wealthy
owner, and their supposed existence

00:23:15.637 --> 00:23:17.378
ignores the command structure of a

00:23:17.438 --> 00:23:21.121
pirate vessel, in which the crew served
for a share of the profit.

00:23:21.737 --> 00:23:25.160
The only pirate ever known to bury
treasure was William Kidd.

00:23:25.941 --> 00:23:31.065
The only treasure so far recovered from
Teach's exploits is that taken from the

00:23:31.085 --> 00:23:32.887
wreckage of what is presumed to be

00:23:32.927 --> 00:23:36.991
the Queen Anne's Revenge, which was found
in 1996.

00:23:37.911 --> 00:23:43.516
As of 2009, more than 250,000 artifacts
have been recovered.

00:23:44.137 --> 00:23:48.541
A selection is on public display at the
North Carolina Maritime Museum.

00:23:49.517 --> 00:23:52.859
Various superstitious tales exist of
Teach's ghost.

00:23:53.559 --> 00:23:57.521
Unexplained lights at sea are often
referred to as Teach's light.

00:23:58.201 --> 00:24:03.503
Some recitals claim that the notorious
pirate now roams the afterlife, searching

00:24:03.543 --> 00:24:04.944
for his head for fear that his

00:24:05.024 --> 00:24:08.046
friends and the devil will not recognize
him.

00:24:08.966 --> 00:24:14.528
A North Carolinian tale holds that Teach's
skull was used as the basis for a silver

00:24:14.609 --> 00:24:15.489
drinking chalice.

00:24:16.125 --> 00:24:20.747
A local judge even claimed to have drunk
from it one night in the 1930s.

00:24:21.687 --> 00:24:26.269
The name of Blackbeard has been attached
to many local attractions, such as

00:24:26.369 --> 00:24:28.110
Charleston's Blackbeard's Cove.

00:24:28.750 --> 00:24:32.432
His name and persona have also featured
heavily in literature.

00:24:32.932 --> 00:24:39.835
He is the main subject of Matilda Douglas'
fictional 1835 work Blackbeard, a page

00:24:39.875 --> 00:24:44.857
from the colonial history of Philadelphia,
film renditions of his life, include

00:24:44.897 --> 00:24:46.739
Blackbeard the Pirates starring

00:24:46.819 --> 00:24:51.823
West Country native Robert Newton, whose
exaggerated West Country accent is

00:24:51.903 --> 00:24:53.625
credited with popularizing the

00:24:53.665 --> 00:25:00.611
stereotypical pirate voice, Blackbeard's
ghost, Blackbeard, Terror at Sea, and the

00:25:00.671 --> 00:25:02.993
Hallmark Channel miniseries Blackbeard.

00:25:03.534 --> 00:25:08.178
Parallels have also been drawn between
Johnson's Blackbeard and the character of

00:25:08.218 --> 00:25:10.339
Captain Jack Sparrow in the adventure

00:25:10.379 --> 00:25:12.021
film Pirates of the Caribbean.

00:25:12.669 --> 00:25:14.170
The Curse of the Black Pearl.

00:25:14.811 --> 00:25:19.795
Speaking of Disney's Pirates of the
Caribbean, no tales of piracy could be

00:25:19.855 --> 00:25:21.276
told without mentioning the

00:25:21.296 --> 00:25:22.597
blockbuster franchise.

00:25:23.338 --> 00:25:28.742
Pirates of the Caribbean is an American
fantasy swashbuckler movie series produced

00:25:28.782 --> 00:25:29.883
by Jerry Bruckheimer

00:25:30.224 --> 00:25:33.366
and based on Walt Disney's theme park ride
of the same name.

00:25:33.927 --> 00:25:38.370
The film series acts as an essential part
of the eponymous pirate's universe.

00:25:38.951 --> 00:25:43.818
Directors of the series include Gore
Verbinski, who directed films 1 through 3,

00:25:44.418 --> 00:25:45.558
Rob Marshall, who

00:25:45.618 --> 00:25:52.000
directed part 4, Joachim Ronning and Espen
Sandberg, who co-directed part 5.

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:57.902
Ted Elliott primarily wrote the series,
and Terry Rossio co-wrote parts 1 through

00:25:58.002 --> 00:25:58.702
4.

00:25:59.002 --> 00:26:04.244
Other writers include Stuart Beattie in
part 1, Jay Walpert in part 1, Jeff

00:26:04.344 --> 00:26:05.984
Nathanson in part 5,

00:26:06.264 --> 00:26:08.125
and Craig Mazin in part 6.

00:26:08.925 --> 00:26:13.389
The tales track the adventures of Captain
Jack Sparrow, portrayed by Johnny Depp,

00:26:14.009 --> 00:26:15.511
Will Turner, portrayed by

00:26:15.631 --> 00:26:20.115
Orlando Bloom, and Elizabeth Swann,
portrayed by Keira Knightley.

00:26:20.535 --> 00:26:25.419
Characters such as Hector Barbosa,
portrayed by Geoffrey Rush, and Joshimi

00:26:25.519 --> 00:26:27.141
Gibbs, portrayed by Kevin

00:26:27.201 --> 00:26:32.284
McNally, follow Jack, Will and Elizabeth
throughout the films.

00:26:33.224 --> 00:26:37.867
Other main characters include James
Norrington, portrayed by Jack Davenport,

00:26:38.287 --> 00:26:40.208
Davy Jones, portrayed by Bill

00:26:40.288 --> 00:26:43.730
Nighy and Tia Dalma, portrayed by Naomi
Harris.

00:26:44.370 --> 00:26:49.313
The fourth film features Blackbeard,
played by Ian McShane and Angelica

00:26:49.393 --> 00:26:50.414
Penelope Cruz.

00:26:51.094 --> 00:26:56.617
The fifth film features Armando Salazar,
played by Gavia Bardem, Henry Turner.

00:26:56.901 --> 00:27:01.886
played by Brenton Thwaites, and Carina
Smith, played by Kea Scodelario.

00:27:02.486 --> 00:27:07.571
The movies take place in a fictionalised
interpretation of the golden age of piracy

00:27:08.052 --> 00:27:09.473
and are set mainly in the

00:27:09.513 --> 00:27:10.213
Caribbean.

00:27:11.315 --> 00:27:17.360
The movie franchise started in 2003 with
Pirates of the Caribbean, The Curse of the

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:18.100
Black Pearl,

00:27:18.421 --> 00:27:22.325
which had a favourable reception from film
critics and audiences.

00:27:22.917 --> 00:27:26.879
It grossed $654 million worldwide.

00:27:27.439 --> 00:27:32.282
After the first film sensation, Walt
Disney Pictures disclosed that a film

00:27:32.402 --> 00:27:33.923
franchise was in the works.

00:27:34.823 --> 00:27:40.967
The second film, subtitled Dead Man's
Chest, came out in 2006 and shattered

00:27:41.027 --> 00:27:41.727
financial

00:27:41.727 --> 00:27:44.689
records worldwide on the day of its
premiere.

00:27:45.489 --> 00:27:52.273
Dead Man's Chest became the top grossing
movie of 2006 with nearly $1.1

00:27:52.293 --> 00:27:54.315
billion at the worldwide box office.

00:27:54.915 --> 00:28:01.541
The third film in the franchise, subtitled
At World's End, followed in 2007, earning

00:28:01.601 --> 00:28:03.042
$960 million.

00:28:04.363 --> 00:28:10.829
Disney released a fourth film, subtitled
On Stranger Tides, in 2011 in conventional

00:28:11.129 --> 00:28:11.829
2D,

00:28:12.050 --> 00:28:14.772
digital 3D, and IMAX 3D.

00:28:15.453 --> 00:28:20.157
On Stranger Tides also took in more than
$1 billion at the box office.

00:28:20.457 --> 00:28:25.639
becoming the second film in the franchise
and only the eighth film in history to do

00:28:25.759 --> 00:28:27.280
this at the time of release.

00:28:27.920 --> 00:28:33.843
A fifth film, subtitled Dead Men Tell No
Tales, was released in 2017.

00:28:34.483 --> 00:28:39.985
The Pirates of the Caribbean franchise has
grossed over $4.5 billion globally.

00:28:40.566 --> 00:28:45.508
It is the 14th highest-grossing movie
series of all time and is the first film

00:28:45.628 --> 00:28:47.409
franchise to produce two

00:28:47.469 --> 00:28:50.674
or more movies you that took in over $1
billion.

00:28:51.514 --> 00:28:55.696
So much money, so much gold, so much
treasure.

00:28:56.276 --> 00:28:59.418
But how would you go about actually
finding real treasures?

00:29:00.298 --> 00:29:02.639
You'd need to become a treasure hunter, of
course.

00:29:03.199 --> 00:29:04.320
What's that, you ask?

00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:05.841
Let's find out.

00:29:06.801 --> 00:29:10.363
Treasure hunting is the physical
investigation of the whereabouts of

00:29:10.423 --> 00:29:11.123
treasure.

00:29:11.563 --> 00:29:17.170
For example, treasure hunters try to find
underwater wreckages and rescue market

00:29:17.230 --> 00:29:18.371
value antiques and

00:29:18.451 --> 00:29:19.151
relics.

00:29:19.552 --> 00:29:22.775
The demand for antiquities typically
motivates this industry.

00:29:23.415 --> 00:29:28.119
The procedure of treasure hunting can be
contentious, as areas such as submerged

00:29:28.199 --> 00:29:30.221
shipwrecks or cultural regions

00:29:30.661 --> 00:29:35.025
could be protected by national or
international regulations interested in

00:29:35.045 --> 00:29:37.407
marine salvage, property ownership,

00:29:37.827 --> 00:29:42.591
commercial diving regulations, sovereign
or state vessels, trade controls,

00:29:42.871 --> 00:29:44.553
protection of cultural heritage.

00:29:45.265 --> 00:29:50.367
Treasure hunting can also refer to
geocaching, a sport in which players use

00:29:50.447 --> 00:29:52.088
GPS units to

00:29:52.128 --> 00:29:57.010
locate hidden stockpiles of trinkets,
toys, or other treasure hunting games.

00:29:57.971 --> 00:29:59.771
What is the history of treasure hunting?

00:30:00.732 --> 00:30:07.235
In 1643, Massachusetts treasure hunter Sir
William Phipps recovered a sunken Spanish

00:30:07.295 --> 00:30:07.995
treasure

00:30:07.995 --> 00:30:12.517
ship that had been shipwrecked on the
Ambrosia Bank in 1599.

00:30:13.149 --> 00:30:19.873
The entire worth of the treasures rescued
came in at 205,536 pounds.

00:30:20.673 --> 00:30:26.977
The Nuestra Señora de Tocha departed
Havana bound for Spain in 1622 before

00:30:27.057 --> 00:30:27.757
shipwrecking.

00:30:28.478 --> 00:30:34.001
American treasure hunter Mel Fisher and
his crew spent 16 years scouring for the

00:30:34.061 --> 00:30:35.302
Nuestra Señora de

00:30:35.462 --> 00:30:36.502
Tocha's remains.

00:30:37.303 --> 00:30:40.825
Three silver bars were located in 1973.

00:30:41.441 --> 00:30:47.343
Five bronze cannons were discovered in
1975, and a wooden hull weighted down by

00:30:47.424 --> 00:30:48.184
ballast rocks,

00:30:48.684 --> 00:30:54.386
iron cannonballs, and antiques in 1980 of
17th century Spain was uncovered,

00:30:54.947 --> 00:30:55.647
documenting

00:30:55.647 --> 00:30:56.807
the site of the shipwreck.

00:30:57.488 --> 00:31:02.550
The SS Central America crashed after
sailing into a hurricane off the Carolina

00:31:02.610 --> 00:31:04.391
coast in 1857.

00:31:04.392 --> 00:31:07.232
In 1987, Thomas G.

00:31:07.412 --> 00:31:10.173
Thompson located the ship's wreck, A.

00:31:10.714 --> 00:31:15.945
ROUV surfaced with over $40 million in
gold from the shipwreck.

00:31:16.474 --> 00:31:23.056
In 1782, the EIC East in Diamond Rovina
sank off the Cape Colony while bearing a

00:31:23.076 --> 00:31:23.776
load of

00:31:23.776 --> 00:31:30.258
2,000 silver ingots, several jewels of
unknown value, and 720 gold ingots.

00:31:30.259 --> 00:31:36.840
In 1952, a British crew retrieved nearly
£1 million worth of the

00:31:36.900 --> 00:31:42.701
£5.3 million in freight, concealed in
Brazil to evade being taxed by the British

00:31:42.761 --> 00:31:43.461
government.

00:31:43.822 --> 00:31:50.356
The SS Laurentic on a journey from
Liverpool to Halifax in 1917, crashed into

00:31:50.376 --> 00:31:51.076
a

00:31:51.076 --> 00:31:54.578
mine and sunk with a gold payload worth 5
million pounds.

00:31:55.318 --> 00:31:59.340
The British Admiralty selected Butenant
Commander G.C.C.

00:32:00.160 --> 00:32:01.741
Damant to recover the ship.

00:32:02.681 --> 00:32:07.783
Damant, assisted by John Haldane, found
the reason and prevention of decompression

